Two separate bugs: (1) opening a variable product modal before `variationMap` is populated showed all variation pills with a diagonal strikethrough and per-pill "Loading" text, and (2) after clearing all browser data, product rails spun indefinitely — requiring a manual refresh to render.

## Bug 1 — Variation pill strikethrough during loading (`ProductVariationSelector`)

`variationsLoading = true` caused `is-disabled dtb-variant-pill--disabled` to be applied to every pill. That class carries a `::after` pseudo-element (`border-top + rotate(-14deg)`) which is the strikethrough. The loading state was visually identical to "sold out / unavailable".

- **`ProductVariationSelector.jsx`**: When `variationsLoading`, apply new `is-loading` class only; skip `is-disabled`/`is-sold-out`/`dtb-variant-pill--disabled` entirely. Remove per-pill "Loading" spinner text. Class logic refactored from nested template-literal ternaries to a `pillClasses[]` array — also eliminates a pre-existing duplicate `dtb-variant-pill--disabled` class that appeared when `soldOut` was true.
- **`product-detail-modern.css`**: Add `.dtb-variant-pill.is-loading { opacity: 0.55; cursor: wait; }` — dimmed with no `::after` line.

## Bug 2 — Catalog infinite loading on fresh cache (`catalog.js`, `productCache.js`, `api/products.js`)

`fetchFromApi()` used bare `fetch()` with no timeout. `apiClient` deduplicates in-flight GETs via a module-level `inflightGetRequests` map — so a single stalled request (slow WP cold-start, poor mobile network) held every component's `getProducts()` promise hostage indefinitely. After a manual refresh the stale in-flight was gone and IndexedDB (written by the first eventually-completed load) served data instantly.

- **`catalog.js`**: Each paginated page request in `fetchFromApi()` now owns an `AbortController` with a 15 s timeout. On abort, the error is normalized to `{ code: 'timeout' }` before bubbling to `loadCatalog()`'s catch, which clears `_cache` and returns `[]`, unblocking all waiters within 15 s.

```js
const controller = new AbortController();
const timeoutId  = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), PAGE_TIMEOUT_MS);
try {
  const result = await proxyFetchProducts(
    { per_page: PER, page, status: 'publish' },
    { signal: controller.signal },
  );
  clearTimeout(timeoutId);
  // ...
} catch (pageErr) {
  clearTimeout(timeoutId);
  const err = pageErr?.name === 'AbortError'
    ? Object.assign(new Error('Catalog page fetch timed out after 15 s.'), { code: 'timeout' })
    : pageErr;
  if (all.length > 0) break;
  throw err;
}
```

- **`api/products.js`**: `fetchProducts(params, options = {})` accepts a second options arg so the `AbortSignal` threads through `apiClient` → `fetch()`.
- **`productCache.js`**: `openDB()` gains a 5 s `setTimeout` reject guard — prevents a blocked IndexedDB connection (another tab holding the DB open post-data-clear) from hanging `readCache()` and consequently `loadCatalog()` forever.

